  #align(center)[*Abstract*]
  #v(0.3em)
  This note extends the Axiomatic Fundamentalism (AF) protocol from a human editorial guideline to a decidable verification calculus over finite typed manuscript graphs. A scientific text is represented as a finite directed derivation graph partitioned into declared boundary premises (Axioms and Modelling Choices), interior derived claims (Consequences, Corollaries, and Demonstrations), and exterior ungrounded symbols. Interior soundness reduces to zero exterior premise flux across the epistemic boundary cut together with acyclicity of the induced dependency graph. A two-pass premise-hoisting operator separates recoverable stylistic factoring defects—midstream modelling assumptions and uncounted continuum imports—from unrecoverable internal contradictions, recording an explicit ontological liability receipt whenever imported state spaces exceed declared finite distinctions. The eight binary checks of the AF Reviewer Rubric form a finite distributive meet-semilattice $bb(B)_8 = ({0, 1}^8, and, bold(1))$, guaranteeing order-independent convergence across parallel and multi-pass audits. For multi-volume treatises, a one-way quotient-graph firewall and cryptographic SHA-256 baseline lock isolate frozen core proofs (`Main`) from active instantiations (`Manual`), while bounded-hop reachability slicing extracts minimal self-contained verification neighborhoods for review. Source-tree provenance isolation extends AF scope discipline to collaborative and multi-agent workflows by quarantining session state, ephemeral scratch paths, and non-regenerable binary dumps from scientific source repositories.
]
